  "id": "ch6-tactics-and-plans",
  "title": "Typical tactics and plans",
  "defaultTags": ["tactics"],
  "intro": "Four positions, each one the end of a line you already know from an earlier chapter. In every case the last move is Black's, and it is the move the whole opening has been building towards. Play through them until the final move looks obvious, because in a real game it has to.\n\n## The two levers\n\nBlack's whole plan in the Caro-Kann comes down to two pawn moves. The first is ...c5, which hits the base of White's pawn chain. The second is ...f6, which hits the head of it. Both need preparation and both need the pieces to be ready to use the lines they open. Everything in between is development.\n\n## Exercise 1: ...f6 in the Advance\n\nWhite's chain runs d4 to e5 and the base has already been traded off, so e5 is a lone pawn with nothing behind it. Black is fully developed, castled, and every piece is looking at the centre. Now the second lever arrives and White has to choose which good square to give up.\n\n## Exercise 2: ...c5 in the main line\n\nOpposite side castling, and it is a race. White is going to push pawns at the black king on the kingside. Black opens a file at the white king on the queenside, and gets there first because the lever was ready before White's was. The move is ...c5 and it should never wait.\n\n## Exercise 3: the c-file in the Exchange\n\nA symmetrical position where neither side has a weakness. What Black has is the half open c-file and a way to use it. The exercise ends with the rook arriving on it, because that is the whole plan and most club players never get round to it.\n\n## Exercise 4: pressure on the isolated pawn\n\nThe Panov gives White an isolated pawn on d4 and a lot of activity to go with it. Black blockades the square in front of it, brings a bishop to the long diagonal and then adds a second attacker to the pawn itself, offering a trade of pieces at the same time. Every trade makes the pawn weaker.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nReaching a good position and then having nothing to do, because ...c5 or ...f6 was never prepared. Opening lines before the pieces are ready to use them, which usually helps whoever has more space. Trading the blockading knight for no reason and setting White's centre free. And playing on the wing where the opponent is stronger rather than in the centre where the game is decided.",
